# No duplication + SonarQube-friendly standards

## No code duplication (DRY)
- Do not copy/paste logic across files/components. Prefer extracting reusable helpers, hooks, shared UI components, or moving logic to existing shared modules.
- Before adding a new util/component, check for an existing equivalent and reuse/extend it.
- Keep one source of truth for constants, types, schemas, selectors, and formatting helpers.
- If duplication is truly unavoidable (e.g., framework constraints), isolate it behind a shared function and keep the duplicated surface minimal; explain the constraint in the PR/commit message (not as code narration comments).

## SonarQube-compatible implementation style
- Keep functions small and readable; reduce nesting with early returns and guard clauses.
- Avoid high cognitive complexity: split complex flows into named helpers and prefer data-driven mappings over long `if/else` chains.
- Prefer explicit error handling: never swallow errors; handle, log (where appropriate), and rethrow/return typed failures.
- Avoid `any`, unsafe non-null assertions (`!`), and overly broad unions; use precise types and narrow safely.
- Avoid duplicated conditions and boolean soup; name predicates and reuse them.
- Do not suppress lint/quality rules unless absolutely necessary; if you must, scope it to the smallest surface area.

## React/TS hygiene (common Sonar hotspots)
- Keep components focused; extract repeated JSX into components.
- Memoize only when needed; avoid creating new inline functions/objects in hot render paths unless harmless.
- Use stable keys, avoid side effects in render, and keep hooks dependency arrays correct.
